# VRYOH Intelligence — System Review & Version 1 Closeout

**Prepared for:** Executive review
**Scope:** Full technical audit of all nine VRYOH workflows (ALA → EIP → ESS → HSI → BRA → RDA, plus the three supporting systems: Google Sheet Sync, SIA, and MRA)
**Purpose:** Document the current state of the system as Version 1 closes, so Version 2 can be scoped with a clear, complete picture of what works, what doesn't, and what to prioritize.

---

## What VRYOH Does

VRYOH takes in guest reviews from six restaurant locations, uses AI to understand what each guest actually felt (beyond the star rating), decides how serious each review is, drafts a response in the guest's own language, and routes everything through a human for final approval before anything goes out. Alongside that, it keeps restaurant managers informed with automated summary emails and trend reports.

The system is built as a chain of nine connected workflows, each handing off to the next. This review examined every one of them, line by line, and cross-checked the results against real production data wherever possible.

---

## The Nine Workflows, One Line Each

| Workflow | What it does | What it hands off |
|---|---|---|
| **ALA** | Ingests the raw review (from a CSV upload), detects language, does a first-pass guess at emotion and topic | A cleaned review record with a preliminary read on what it's about |
| **EIP** | Takes a real, deep look — matches the review against a full emotional and pain-point reference library, in the guest's own language | A structured emotional and topical classification |
| **ESS** | Checks that classification for internal consistency, then assesses *how* the guest expressed themselves (directly, sarcastically, holding back, etc.) | A validated signal plus an "expression style" read |
| **HSI** | Interprets what the signal actually means for the business — the human story behind the data — and sets an initial severity tier | A plain-language interpretation and a severity tier (routine / needs care / urgent) |
| **BRA** | Decides the response *strategy* — tone, urgency, and any governance concerns — without writing anything yet | A response brief, or a hard stop if something's too risky to proceed automatically |
| **RDA** | Actually writes the draft response, in the guest's language, following the brand's voice, then runs its own quality check | A finished draft, held for human approval |
| **Google Sheet Sync** | Copies pending drafts into each restaurant's shared spreadsheet so a manager can review and approve them | An approval queue managers actually work from |
| **SIA** | Runs on a schedule (not per-review) and groups recent signals into patterns and trends across each location | Trend data used in reports |
| **MRA** | Also scheduled, not per-review — builds and sends the daily/weekly/monthly summary emails to each restaurant | The actual client-facing report emails |

