## The Good News

The system's core design is sound, and several of its most important safety principles are genuinely working, not just documented:

- **The "human always has the final say" principle is real, not just a slogan.** Multiple independent checkpoints — some automated, some clearly working as intended — correctly flag reviews for human review when something needs a closer look, and this was confirmed directly against real examples during this audit.
- **The system never lets AI make business promises on its own.** Discounts, refunds, and similar commitments are explicitly and repeatedly blocked from ever appearing in a drafted response.
- **Cost efficiency is built in thoughtfully.** Routine, low-severity reviews are handled without an AI call at all wherever possible, reserving the more expensive reasoning steps for cases that actually need judgment.
- **The bilingual (English/Spanish) capability is real and, in the drafting stage particularly, was built with genuine care** — in some respects the Spanish version of the response-writing logic is more refined than the English one.
- One major, previously-reported problem — Spanish-language reviews appearing not to process correctly — was tracked down to its root cause during this review and has been fixed and verified.

---

## What Needs Attention

Issues found during this review fall into four groups, roughly by how urgently they should be addressed.

### 1. Things that are visibly wrong to clients or staff today

- **One of the automated weekly client emails currently states a performance number ("100% Response Coverage") that isn't calculated from anything — it's a fixed placeholder that was never replaced with a real calculation.** Every client sees this every week regardless of actual performance. This is the single highest-priority fix identified in this review.
- **A meaningful chunk of the performance numbers reported to clients — response speed, on-time rate, and SEO effectiveness — are structurally guaranteed to always show as zero.** This traces back to an earlier, deliberate change to how approvals are tracked internally; the reporting logic that calculates these numbers was never updated to match, so it's been quietly reporting zero instead of erroring out.
- **A garbled character sequence sometimes appears in guest review text shown to staff** (a star-rating symbol that gets corrupted somewhere during file intake). It's cosmetic — it doesn't affect the actual star rating or the AI's understanding of the review — but it's visible in real reports today and worth a clean fix at the source.
- One drafted response reviewed during this audit had a small punctuation error ("Cheers!,") that would be visible to a client reading their sent response.

### 2. A few things that need to be verified before they're trusted

- **The most important finding of this review**: there is strong evidence that an entire parallel Spanish-language response-drafting system — built with real engineering effort — may not actually be switched on. The two paths (English and Spanish) were reviewed in detail, and the "on switch" for Spanish appears, from the code, to lead nowhere. This needs a direct, real-world test before anything else, since if true, it means Spanish-language responses may not be getting drafted at all despite the system clearly being built to handle them.
- **A repeat-guest detection feature likely isn't working**, based on how a comparison in the code is written. This affects whether the system correctly recognizes when a guest has reviewed before — worth a live test to confirm.
- **One system-wide safety check (the final backstop that's supposed to block discriminatory or legally risky drafted responses before anything reaches a client) has no record-keeping if it ever fires.** It's very likely working, but if it ever activates, there would currently be no way to know it happened or why, other than digging through raw system logs. Given this is the single most safety-critical control in the whole pipeline, it deserves the same visibility as every other safeguard.
- The system's own internal documentation admits that its multi-client processing loop (making sure every restaurant location gets processed in each scheduled run, not just the first one) has never been tested against real, live multi-client data. A specific, related question was found during this review that should be resolved before this goes unchallenged any longer.

### 3. Consistency gaps between English and Spanish

Several safety and quality checks that exist for English reviews don't have a Spanish equivalent yet — most notably, a safeguard designed to catch inappropriate financial promises (like refund offers) in drafted responses only scans for English trigger words, meaning the same protection doesn't exist for Spanish drafts. Similarly, one severity-classification calculation used for daily reports doesn't fully account for all the categories of concern the system is capable of detecting — including its most serious category.

### 4. Smaller technical debt

A number of smaller items were found — leftover placeholder code that never got finished, a handful of steps that do nothing and could be safely removed, a couple of places where an outdated internal label is still referenced even though it no longer applies, and a security best-practice recommendation around how one access token gets generated. None of these are urgent on their own, but they add up, and cleaning them up will make the system easier to maintain and extend in Version 2.

